#---
# purpose: "the String module: byte operations, codepoints and search, checked against Elixir"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # string_surface
#
# fz had no String module at all -- `Utf8` (4 functions) was the entire text
# surface. This is the core of one, and what makes it WRITABLE IN fz rather
# than in Rust is the work that came before it: almost every function here is a
# byte scanner, and until recently a byte scanner was quadratic, because
# matching `<<c, rest :: binary>>` copied the whole remainder at every step
# (fz-5xp.55) and a segment whose size was computed could not be matched at all
# (fz-5xp.54).
#
# So the module declares exactly TWO primitives beyond what `Kernel` already
# gives it -- `byte_size/1`, which is a header read, and `to_atom/1`, which
# must reach the node's atom table. Everything else is ordinary fz recursion
# over a binary.
#
# CASE MAPPING IS FULL UNICODE, matching Elixir's default mode -- `"straße"`
# upcases to `"STRASSE"`, which is one of the 103 special casings where one
# codepoint becomes several. The table lives in the runtime rather than in fz
# source: 2,723 codepoint pairs written as fz clauses would go through the
# dispatch matrix and make compile work proportional to the TABLE rather than to
# the program, which is the opposite of what fz is for (fz-5xp.68).
#
# `to_byte_list/1` is NOT Elixir's `to_charlist/1`. Elixir's returns a charlist,
# which `inspect` renders as `~c"abc"`; charlists are deferred project-wide, so
# fz has no such type. The value is the same list of integers, and the name says
# so rather than claiming a parity it cannot show -- the oracle asks Elixir for
# `charlists: :as_lists` to compare the values at all.
def main() do
  dbg(String.length("héllo"))
  dbg(String.starts_with?("hello", "he"))
  dbg(String.starts_with?("hello", "xx"))
  dbg(String.ends_with?("hello", "lo"))
  dbg(String.byte_slice("hello world", 6, 5))
  dbg(String.replace_prefix("hello", "he", "HE"))
  dbg(String.replace_suffix("hello", "lo", "LO"))
  dbg(String.replace_leading("aaabc", "a", "X"))
  dbg(String.replace_trailing("abccc", "c", "Y"))
  dbg(String.trim("  hi  "))
  dbg(String.trim_leading("  hi"))
  dbg(String.trim_trailing("hi  "))
  dbg(String.upcase("héllo"))
  dbg(String.upcase("straße"))
  dbg(String.downcase("HÉLLO"))
  dbg(String.downcase("İ"))
  dbg(String.duplicate("ab", 3))
  dbg(String.contains?("hello", "ell"))
  dbg(String.count("banana", "an"))
  dbg(String.split("a,b,c", ","))
  dbg(String.replace("a-b-c", "-", "+"))
  dbg(String.codepoints("héllo"))
  dbg(String.next_codepoint("héllo"))
  dbg(String.valid?("hi"))
  dbg(String.to_byte_list("abc"))
  dbg(String.reverse("héllo"))
  dbg(String.first("héllo"))
  dbg(String.last("héllo"))

  # numbers, which Elixir's String parses strictly: `to_float/1` REQUIRES a
  # decimal point, so `"1"` raises where `"1.0"` is 1.0
  dbg(String.to_integer("42"))
  dbg(String.to_integer("-7"))
  dbg(String.to_float("2.5"))
  dbg(String.to_float("-0.25"))

  # printability, and the two functions that have to reason about INVALID
  # UTF-8 rather than assume it away
  dbg(String.printable?("héllo"))
  dbg(String.printable?(<<0>>))
  dbg(String.replace_invalid(<<104, 255, 105>>, "?"))
  dbg(String.chunk("hi"))
  dbg(String.chunk(<<104, 255, 105>>))

  # trimming is over the UNICODE whitespace set, not the ASCII one -- the
  # ideographic space below is U+3000, and it is the small table Elixir owns
  dbg(String.trim("　hi　"))

  # the grapheme seam: a grapheme IS a codepoint until UAX #29 lands, which is
  # correct outside combining marks, regional indicators and emoji sequences
  dbg(String.graphemes("héllo"))
  dbg(String.next_grapheme("héllo"))
end
